    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d152">

                <pb facs="0194" n="180" xml:id="pg180"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">839.415/141b: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Ambassador in Haiti</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >White</hi>)<anchor n="31" xml:id="d152-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-02-14T20:00:00-04:00">February 14,
                            1944—8 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>48. The President desires you to stress to Lescot his personal interest in the
                    border meeting with Trujillo. The President considers it essential that this
                    meeting take place during the Dominican Centennial festivities.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Stettinius</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d152-f1" n="31" type="source" xml:id="d152-f1-content">
                <p>This telegram was sent at the request of Charles Taussig following his interview
                    the same day with President Roosevelt. Mr. Taussig was chairman of the United
                    States Section, Anglo-American Caribbean Commission, and chairman designate of
                    the United States delegation to the Dominican Centennial.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
